{
  "decline_reason": "I cannot say how your particular application would be decided. The passages explain the treatment of actual confusion in honest concurrent use evidence, but do not establish the outcome for your application.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "If you are relying on honest concurrent use, the Manual says you should provide information about actual confusion incidents if it is available, although such evidence is not usually provided to the examiner.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/3",
          "quote": "It is not usual for evidence of instances of confusion to be provided to the examiner. However, the applicant should provide this information if it is available."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If you do not know of any incidents, the Manual says a statement to that effect in your declaration will be taken at face value unless other circumstances cast doubt on its accuracy.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/3",
          "quote": "In most cases, mention of this issue consists of a brief denial of knowledge of any instances contained within the applicant's declaration. Such information, when incorporated in a declaration, will be taken at face value, unless other circumstances cast doubt on its accuracy."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual, actual confusion bears directly on the assessment of honesty and strengthens concerns about the likely degree of confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/3",
          "quote": "In the unlikely event that confusion is shown to have occurred, it will have a direct bearing on the consideration of honesty and will undoubtedly add force to the concern regarding the likely degree of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Actual confusion does not necessarily mean the application cannot proceed.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/3",
          "quote": "However, the discovery of instances of actual confusion is not necessarily fatal to the application."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For honest concurrent use, the Manual places the onus on the applicant to establish that their use was honest.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~1",
          "quote": "The applicant, seeking to establish honest concurrent use, bears the onus of proof and must establish that their use was honest."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 44(3) permits the Registrar to accept an application where satisfied of honest concurrent use or that other circumstances make acceptance proper, subject to conditions or limitations the Registrar thinks fit.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(3) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied: (a) that there has been honest concurrent use of the 2 trade marks; or (b) that, because of other circumstances, it is proper to do so; the Registrar may accept the application for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark subject to any conditions or limitations that the Registrar thinks fit to impose."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "When considering acceptance because of other circumstances, the Manual directs examiners to assess the evidence and circumstances for and against acceptance holistically, rather than treating any one circumstance in isolation.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/4/28/4/2",
          "quote": "When an examiner is contemplating whether an application should be accepted because of ‘other circumstances’ they should holistically consider the evidence and the totality of circumstances, both for and against, to determine whether it is proper to accept the application in the face of a real and tangible danger of confusion. Some circumstances may be more compelling than others, but no circumstance should be considered in isolation."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
